Q: Statically verify a undo function is called A: <h3>Murphy not Machiavelli</h3>
<p>A general theme in software is that you can only protect against Murphy, not Machiavelli.</p>
<p>That is, you should aim for a <em>Pit of Success</em> effect, by making it easy (low-effort, automatic) to do the right thing, and hard to do the wrong thing... but you can never prevent a user who <em>really</em> wants to to shoot themselves in the foot.</p>
<h3>Drop</h3>
<p>The most idiomatic way is to call <code>undo</code> in the <code>Drop</code> implementation of a value:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn foo() -&gt; Undo {}

struct Undo {}

impl Drop for Undo {
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        println!(&quot;undo foo&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called <em>unless</em> the user calls <code>mem::forget</code> or leaks it or whatever.</li>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called with the same arguments as used when calling <code>foo</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Disadvantages:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>undo</code> may be called <em>too soon</em>, if the user forgets to assign the result of <code>foo</code> to a variable for example.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Guard Drop</h3>
<p>The most idiomatic way to call <code>undo</code> <em>after</em> a user is done is to provide a <em>guard</em> which will mediate all &quot;accesses&quot;, then undo in its <code>Drop</code> implementation.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn foo() -&gt; Guard {}

struct Guard {}

impl Guard {
    fn frobnicate(&amp;self) {}
}

impl Drop for Guard {
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        println!(&quot;undo foo&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called <em>unless</em> the user calls <code>mem::forget</code> or leaks it or whatever.</li>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called with the same arguments as used when calling <code>foo</code>.</li>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called in a timely manner, as the user must keep the guard alive to do any protected operation.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Note: this is the technique used by <code>RefCell</code> or <code>Mutex</code> in the standard library; it's that idiomatic.</em></p>
<h3>Scope</h3>
<p>A less flexible solution is to use a scoped implementation:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn with_foo&lt;R, F&gt;(callback: F) -&gt; R
where
    F: UnwindSafe + FnOnce() -&gt; R,
{
    //  An alternative implementation would use the Guard Drop pattern internally
    //  to avoid catch + resume.

    foo();

    let result = panic::catch_unwind(callback);

    undo_foo();

    match result {
        Ok(result) =&gt; result,
        Err(error) =&gt; panic::resume_unwind(error),
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called, unless panic aborts.</li>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called with the same arguments as used when calling <code>foo</code>.</li>
<li><code>undo</code> will be called in a timely manner.</li>
</ul>
<p>Disadvantages:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inflexible: the user cannot break down their callback into multiple pieces, for asynchronous processing for example.</li>
<li>Not async compatible: you would need an async variant for the user to supply an async callback.</li>
<li>Rightward drift: the user must essentially use a closure, creating a rightward drift. In particular, this composes poorly if the user needs to use <code>with_bar</code> and <code>with_baz</code> as well...</li>
</ul>
<p>Arguably, some of the disadvantages can be seen as advantages. If a lock on a resource is grabbed for the duration of the operation, then forcing the user to minimize the scope during which the lock is held can avoid letting the user shooting themselves in the foot.</p>
